          "serviceType": "Comprehensive new patient dental exam",
          "url": "https://www.livewelldentalva.com/new-patient/first-visit-new-exam"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://www.livewelldentalva.com/new-patient/first-visit-new-exam#faq",
          "@type": "FAQPage",
          "isPartOf": {
            "@id": "https://www.livewelldentalva.com/new-patient/first-visit-new-exam#webpage"
          },
          "mainEntity": [
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Complete your forms, bring your photo ID and insurance card, and make a list of any medications, dental concerns, or goals you want to discuss. If you have questions about coverage, you can review our insurance and financing information before your visit."
              },
              "name": "How do I prepare for my first visit?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Most first visits take longer than a routine cleaning because we take time for digital records, a complete exam, and a conversation about your goals. The goal is to understand your oral health clearly before recommending any next steps."
              },
              "name": "How long does a new patient exam take?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Sometimes, but not always. If your gums and teeth are healthy enough for a routine cleaning, we may be able to complete it. If you need a deeper type of care or more evaluation first, we will explain why before scheduling the next step."
              },
              "name": "Will I get a cleaning at my first visit?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Tell us before or during your visit. We will move at a pace that feels comfortable, explain each step before it happens, and give you time to ask questions. If anxiety has kept you away from the dentist, we can also discuss sedation dentistry."
              },
              "name": "What if I have dental anxiety?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Yes, when needed. Digital X-rays help us check for cavities, bone levels, infections, and problems that may not be visible during the exam. We only take the records needed to understand your health and plan your care properly."
              },
              "name": "Do you take X-rays at the first visit?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Let us know when you schedule. If you have pain, swelling, a broken tooth, or another urgent concern, we will focus on helping you understand what is happening and what your options are. You can also visit our dental emergencies page for urgent dental concerns."
              },
              "name": "What if I am coming in because of pain or a broken tooth?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Dr. Chris will review what we found, explain what is healthy, what needs attention, and where to start. You will leave with a clearer plan, not pressure to make every decision at once."
              },
              "name": "What happens after the exam?"
            }
          ]
